The CLI tool takes the name of the directory as a command-line argument and calls the appropriate function based on whether the directory exists or not. If the directory does not exist, it creates a new one and sets an environment variable to its path. If the directory exists, it updates it with a new UUID identifier and sets an environment variable to its path.

To handle concurrent access gracefully, the `check_directory` function uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can modify the directory at a time. To prevent abuse or excessive load on the file system, the `create_directory` and `update_directory` functions use a rate-limiting mechanism to limit the number of directory creations or updates per second.

To handle external configuration service failures gracefully, the CLI tool retries failed requests up to 3 times before returning an error. To ensure robustness under high load, the `check_directory` function uses a thread pool to parallelize the check process and ensures that each directory creation or update is atomic.

To optimize performance, the `create_directory` and `update_directory` functions use `File::create()` instead of `std::fs::create_dir()` to minimize file system operations. The `check_directory` function uses a hash map to store recently accessed directories and their UUID identifiers, reducing the number of directory lookups required.

To handle sensitive information securely, the CLI tool uses `Command::new("export")` to set environment variables securely. The `generate_directory_name` function uses a secure UUID library to generate unique directory names with high entropy.

To ensure user-friendliness and provide clear feedback on actions, the CLI tool provides clear error messages for failed operations and returns successful status messages upon completion.']

['Here is a sample C++ implementation of a distributed tree structure that supports both DFS and BFS traversal operations:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <queue>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

struct Node {
    string key;
    vector<Node*> children;
};

class Tree {
private:
    unordered_map<string, Node*> nodes_;
    mutex lock_;
public:
    void insert(const string& key) {
        unique_lock<mutex> lk(lock_);
        if (nodes_.find(key) == nodes_.end()) {
            nodes_.insert({key, new Node()});
        }
    }

    void remove(const string& key) {
        unique_lock<mutex> lk(lock_);
        if (nodes_.find(key) != nodes_.end()) {
            delete nodes_[key];
            nodes_.erase(key);
        }
    }

    void traverseDFS(const string& key, function<void(Node*)> callback) {
        unique_lock<mutex> lk(lock_);
        if (nodes_.find(key) != nodes_.end()) {
            Node* node = nodes_[key];
            for (auto child : node->children) {
                traverseDFS(child->key, callback);
            }
            callback(node);
        }
    }

    void traverseBFS(const string& key, function<void(Node*)> callback) {
        unique_lock<mutex> lk(lock_);
        if (nodes_.find(key) != nodes_.end()) {
            Node* node = nodes_[key];
            queue<Node*> q;
            q.push(node);
            while (!q.empty()) {
                Node* current = q.front();
                callback(current);
                for (auto child : current->children) {
                    q.push(child);
                }
                q.pop();
            }
        }
    }

    void update(const string& key, const vector<string>& children) {
        unique_lock<mutex> lk(lock_);
        if (nodes_.find(key) != nodes_.end()) {
            Node* node = nodes_[key];
            for (auto child : children) {
                insert(child);
                node->children.push_back(nodes_[child]);
            }
        }
    }
};
```
The `Tree` class maintains a map of keys to `Node` objects, and provides methods for inserting, removing, traversing the tree in DFS and BFS orders, and updating the children of a node. The `insert`, `remove`, `traverseDFS`, and `traverseBFS` methods are thread-safe using a mutex lock. The `update` method also uses a mutex lock to ensure atomicity of the operation.

To optimize memory allocation patterns, the tree structure can be partitioned into smaller subtrees that can be stored in different memory pools. This can help reduce fragmentation and improve cache locality during traversal operations.

The `Tree` class can also be integrated with an external cloud-based storage service for persisting the tree structure. This would enable efficient backups and recovery, as well as support for distributed caching mechanisms.

To handle concurrent invocations from various threads, the class can use memory pools and distributed locking mechanisms to ensure thread safety and robust error handling. The `Tree` class can also implement detailed logging for monitoring execution metrics, including start time, end time, and error details.']

