* Plain text: This is the simplest format that just prints out the nodes and edges in a straightforward way. It is useful for simple graphs where you don't need to show any additional information.
* LaTeX: This format uses LaTeX commands to draw the graph on a page. It is useful for creating documents with diagrams or other graphical content.
* GraphViz: This format uses the GraphViz software to create a diagram of the graph. It is useful for creating more complex diagrams that need to show additional information about the nodes and edges.
* Markdown: This format uses Markdown syntax to create tables and lists that represent the graph. It is useful for creating documentation or other text content that needs to include information about the nodes and edges of the graph.
* HTML: This format uses HTML tags to create a webpage that represents the graph. It is useful for creating web pages that need to show a diagram of the graph.

The program also allows the user to specify the input file and the output file. If no output file is specified, the program will use the default output file `output.txt`.']

