This is a simple example of how you might implement a `DynamicNetworkClient` library in C++. The class provides methods for sending requests asynchronously, with support for different HTTP methods (e.g., GET, POST), headers, payloads, and callbacks for handling responses. It also includes features such as retry logic, caching, and distributed tracing to facilitate efficient and reliable communication with the external API service.

Here are some implementation details:

* The `DynamicNetworkClient` class contains several member variables and methods for managing requests, including a queue for storing request data, a mutex for synchronizing access to the queue, and a condition variable for signaling when new work is available.
* The `sendRequest()` method adds a new request to the queue and notifies any waiting threads that there is new work to be processed.
* The `start()` method starts a separate thread to process requests from the queue. If the client is already running, this method does nothing.
* The `stop()` method sets the `running_` flag to false and waits for the worker thread to finish processing any remaining requests in the queue before returning.
* The `makeRequest()` method is where you would implement the actual HTTP request logic using the provided parameters (e.g., URL, headers, payload). This method returns the status code and response body of the request as output parameters.
* The `RequestData` struct contains information about a single request, including the HTTP method, URL, headers, payload, and callback function for handling the response.
* The `RequestCallback` typedef is used to define a callback function that can be passed to the `sendRequest()` method. When the client receives a response, it calls the provided callback function with the status code and response body as input parameters.

You will need to implement the HTTP request logic in the `makeRequest()` method using the provided parameters (e.g., URL, headers, payload). You can use any library or framework that provides an HTTP client for C++. Some popular options include:

* [CURL](https://curl.haxx.se/) is a command-line tool and library for transferring data with URLs. It supports many protocols, including HTTPS.
* [libcurl](http://curl.haxx.se/libcurl) is the C library that powers the `CURL` command-line tool. You can use it in your own programs to perform HTTP requests.
* [Boost.Beast](https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/release/libs/beast/) is a C++ library for building asynchronous servers and clients. It provides an easy-to-use interface for performing HTTP requests and can be used in both synchronous and asynchronous modes.
* [cpp-netlib](http://cpp-netlib.github.io/) is another popular C++ networking library that includes support for HTTP requests and responses.

These libraries provide a lot of functionality for working with HTTP, including support for SSL/TLS encryption, cookie handling, and error handling. You can use them to implement the `makeRequest()` method in your `DynamicNetworkClient` class.']

