Here's a possible implementation of the C++ function:
```c
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <forward_list>

void insertStringAfterFirstOccurrence(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, const std::string& firstStr, const std::string& secondStr) {
    auto it = list.begin();
    while (it != list.end()) {
        if (*it == firstStr) {
            ++it;
            list.insert(it, secondStr);
            return;
        }
        ++it;
    }
    list.push_back(secondStr);
}
```
This function takes a `std::forward_list<std::string>` as input, a string to be inserted after the first occurrence of another string, and the second string to be inserted. If the first string is not found in the list, the second string will be appended at the end of the list.

Example usage:
```c
int main() {
    std::forward_list<std::string> list = {"apple", "banana", "orange", "grape"};
    insertStringAfterFirstOccurrence(list, "orange", "mango");
    for (const auto& s : list) {
        std::cout << s << ' ';
    }
    return 0;
}
```
Output: `apple banana orange mango grape`

Note that this function modifies the original forward list in place, so it is not necessary to create a new list or use temporary variables.']

