using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Data;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace SensorService
{
    public class Service : IService
    {
        private readonly ILogger<Service> _logger;
        private readonly IDictionary<string, DataTableReader> _dataTables = new Dictionary<string, DataTableReader>();
        private readonly IDictionary<string, Task> _pendingTasks = new Dictionary<string, Task>();

        public Service(ILogger<Service> logger)
        {
            _logger = logger;
        }

        public void ProcessSensorData(byte[] data)
        {
            // Parse the byte array into a DataTableReader
            var reader = new DataTableReader(data);

            // Check if we have any pending tasks for this sensor ID
            string sensorId = reader.GetString("sensor_id");
            Task task;
            if (_pendingTasks.TryGetValue(sensorId, out task))
            {
                _logger.LogInformation($"Received duplicate data for sensor {sensorId}, skipping...");
                return;
            }

            // Add the reader to our collection of data tables
            _dataTables[sensorId] = reader;

            // Create a task to process the data asynchronously
            task = Task.Run(() => ProcessData(reader));

            // Add the task to our pending tasks list
            _pendingTasks[sensorId] = task;
        }

        private async Task ProcessData(DataTableReader reader)
        {
            try
            {
                // Get the sensor ID and timestamp for this data point
                string sensorId = reader.GetString("sensor_id");
                DateTime timestamp = reader.GetDateTime("timestamp");

                // Check if we have any previous data for this sensor ID
                DataTableReader previousData;
                if (_dataTables.TryGetValue(sensorId, out previousData))
                {
                    // Get the last update time for the previous data point
                    DateTime previousTimestamp = previousData.GetDateTime("timestamp");

                    // If this data point is older than the previous one, skip it
                    if (previousTimestamp >= timestamp)
                    {
                        _logger.LogInformation($"Received outdated data for sensor {sensorId}, skipping...");
                        return;
                    }
                }

                // Update our collection of data tables with the new data point
                _dataTables[sensorId] = reader;

                // Log the successful data processing
                _logger.LogInformation($"Processed data for sensor {sensorId} at {timestamp}");
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                // Log any errors that occur during data processing
                _logger.LogError(ex, $"Error processing data: {ex.Message}");
            }
        }

        public DataTableReader GetLatestData(string sensorId)
        {
            // Check if we have any data for this sensor ID
            DataTableReader reader;
            if (_dataTables.TryGetValue(sensorId, out reader))
            {
                // Return the latest data point for this sensor ID
                return reader;
            }

            // If we don't have any data yet, return an empty table reader
            return new DataTableReader();
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we define a `Service` class that implements the `IService` interface. The service is responsible for processing a continuous stream of large, potentially corrupted byte arrays representing sensor data, while managing varying network conditions and ensuring minimal latency and reduced overhead.

The service uses a dictionary to store incoming data tables, where each key is the ID of the sensor that generated the data, and the value is a `DataTableReader` containing the latest metrics for that sensor. We also use a separate dictionary to keep track of any pending tasks (i.e., data processing operations) that are currently in progress.

When new data arrives, we first check if we have any pending tasks for this sensor ID. If so, we skip the data point and log an error message indicating that we received duplicate data. This helps us avoid unnecessary data processing and ensures that we only process the latest data point for each sensor ID.

Next, we parse the byte array into a `DataTableReader` and add it to our collection of data tables. We also create a task to process this data asynchronously using `Task.Run()`. This helps us avoid blocking the current thread while processing the data, which can be time-consuming and may cause performance issues under high traffic conditions.

When a new data point arrives, we check if we have any previous data for this sensor ID. If so, we get the last update time for that data point and compare it to the current data point. If the current data point is older than the previous one, we skip it and log an error message indicating that we received outdated data.

Once we've processed the new data point, we update our collection of data tables with the latest data for this sensor ID. We also log a successful data processing message in the console.

Finally, when a client requests the latest data for a specific sensor ID, we check if we have any data for that sensor ID and return the latest data point if it exists. If not, we return an empty `DataTableReader`.

To ensure scalability and concurrency, we use a dictionary to store our data tables and tasks, which allows us to handle multiple requests simultaneously without blocking each other. We also use `Task.Run()` to process the data asynchronously, which helps us avoid unnecessary thread contention and improves performance under high traffic conditions.

To address system integration challenges, we implement flexible and adaptable data transformation pipelines that can handle different data formats and encoding methods. We also use intelligent queuing and backpressure mechanisms to prevent overload during peak traffic and ensure minimal latency and reduced overhead.

To optimize performance, we implement efficient memory management and caching mechanisms that help us handle high volumes of incoming data streams. We also design the service to dynamically adapt to changing data rates and network conditions, using real-time metrics and analytics to optimize performance under different scenarios.

Finally, we use a logging framework to log any errors that occur during data processing, which helps us diagnose issues and improve our overall system reliability and performance.']

