The `setMode`, `write`, and `read` functions in the `GPIO` and `I2C` classes are pure virtual functions that must be implemented by any derived class. The `GPIOI2CController` class provides concrete implementations of these functions, which call through to the appropriate function in one of its internal GPIO or I2C objects based on the pin number provided.

The `setAddress` and `write` functions in the `I2C` class are also pure virtual functions that must be implemented by any derived class. The `GPIOI2CController` class provides concrete implementations of these functions, which call through to the appropriate function in one of its internal I2C objects based on the address provided.

The `read` function in the `I2C` class is a pure virtual function that must be implemented by any derived class. The `GPIOI2CController` class provides a default implementation of this function, which calls through to the appropriate function in one of its internal I2C objects based on the address provided.

The destructor for the `GPIOI2CController` class deletes all of its internal GPIO and I2C objects when it is destroyed. This ensures that any memory allocated by these objects is properly released.

