Here is an example of how you could implement a robust, scalable semaphore manager in C++ that supports creating, deleting, acquiring, and releasing semaphores with specified initial values and names:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

class SemaphoreManager {
private:
    // Map of semaphore names to their corresponding semaphores
    std::map<std::string, std::shared_ptr<Semaphore>> semaphores;

public:
    // Add a new semaphore with the given name and initial value
    void addSemaphore(const std::string& name, int initialValue) {
        if (semaphores.find(name) != semaphores.end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("A semaphore with this name already exists");
        }
        semaphores[name] = std::make_shared<Semaphore>(initialValue);
    }

    // Remove a semaphore with the given name
    void removeSemaphore(const std::string& name) {
        if (semaphores.find(name) == semaphores.end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("No semaphore with this name exists");
        }
        semaphores.erase(name);
    }

    // Acquire a semaphore with the given name
    void acquireSemaphore(const std::string& name) {
        if (semaphores.find(name) == semaphores.end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("No semaphore with this name exists");
        }
        std::shared_ptr<Semaphore> semaphore = semaphores[name];
        if (semaphore->availablePermits() > 0) {
            --semaphore->availablePermits();
            return true;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }

    // Release a semaphore with the given name
    void releaseSemaphore(const std::string& name) {
        if (semaphores.find(name) == semaphores.end()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("No semaphore with this name exists");
        }
        std::shared_ptr<Semaphore> semaphore = semaphores[name];
        ++semaphore->availablePermits();
    }
};

class Semaphore {
private:
    // The number of available permits for the semaphore
    int availablePermits;

public:
    // Constructor that takes an initial value for the semaphore
    explicit Semaphore(int initialValue) : availablePermits(initialValue) {}

    // Get the current number of available permits for the semaphore
    int availablePermits() {
        return availablePermits;
    }
};
```
This implementation uses a `std::map` to store the semaphores by their names, and each semaphore is represented as an instance of the `Semaphore` class. The `addSemaphore`, `removeSemaphore`, `acquireSemaphore`, and `releaseSemaphore` methods are used to add/remove, acquire/release a semaphore with the given name, respectively.

To implement error handling and recovery strategies, you could use try-catch blocks to catch any exceptions that may be thrown when adding or removing a semaphore, or when acquiring or releasing a permit from a semaphore. You could also use redundancy mechanisms such as replication of the semaphore data across multiple servers, and/or using load balancing to distribute the workload across multiple servers.

To ensure secure communication between the semaphore manager and external systems, you could use encryption and authentication protocols such as SSL/TLS or OAuth2, which would provide confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity of the data transmitted between the manager and the external systems. Additionally, you could implement rate limiting to prevent DDoS attacks and protect against distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks.

To modularize the codebase, you could separate concerns such as logging, semaphore management, and integration with external systems into different modules or libraries, allowing for independent scaling and maintenance of these components. This would also allow for easier updates to each module without affecting overall system stability.

To integrate with an external monitoring system, you could use a distributed message broker such as Apache Kafka or RabbitMQ to publish events related to semaphore operations, which could trigger automated responses such as scaling the number of available permits in a semaphore pool or sending notifications to operational teams. This would allow for real-time insights into semaphore usage and provide non-disruptive updates to the system without affecting ongoing operations.

Regarding the method that takes a byte array as input and returns an object of a specific class using serialization techniques, you could use the `boost::serialization` library to serialize/deserialize objects in C++. Here's an example implementation:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <boost/archive/binary_iarchive.hpp>
#include <boost/archive/binary_oarchive.hpp>
#include <boost/serialization/export.hpp>
#include <boost/serialization/base_object.hpp>

class Object {
public:
    virtual ~Object() {}
};

BOOST_CLASS_EXPORT(Object)

class MyClass : public Object {
public:
    // Constructor that takes a string and initializes the member variable
    explicit MyClass(const std::string& str) : str_(str) {}

    // Getter method for the member variable
    const std::string& getStr() const { return str_; }

private:
    std::string str_;
};

void serializeObject(const Object* object, std::vector<char>& bytes) {
    boost::archive::binary_oarchive oa(bytes);
    oa << object;
}

std::unique_ptr<Object> deserializeObject(const std::vector<char>& bytes) {
    boost::archive::binary_iarchive ia(bytes);
    Object* object = nullptr;
    ia >> object;
    return std::unique_ptr<Object>(object);
}

int main() {
    // Create an instance of MyClass and serialize it
    MyClass obj("Hello, world!");
    std::vector<char> bytes;
    serializeObject(&obj, bytes);

    // Deserialize the object from the byte array
    auto deserializedObj = deserializeObject(bytes);

    // Print the string member variable of the deserialized object
    std::cout << "Deserialized object: " << ((MyClass*)deserializedObj.get())->getStr() << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```
This implementation uses the `boost::serialization` library to serialize/deserialize objects in C++. The `Object` class is inherited by the `MyClass` class, which has a member variable of type `std::string`. The `serializeObject` function takes an object pointer and a byte array reference as input, and serializes the object into the byte array using the `boost::archive::binary_oarchive` output archive. The `deserializeObject` function takes a byte array reference and returns a unique pointer to the deserialized object using the `boost::archive::binary_iarchive` input archive. Finally, the main function creates an instance of `MyClass`, serializes it into a byte array, deserializes it back from the byte array, and prints the string member variable of the deserialized object.']

