To implement a highly configurable logging framework in C++, we can use a combination of libraries such as boost::log, log4cplus, or spdlog. These libraries provide a flexible and modular way to handle logging, with support for multiple log levels, output targets, and error handling mechanisms.

Here is an example implementation using boost::log:
```cpp
#include <boost/log/core.hpp>
#include <boost/log/sources/logger.hpp>
#include <boost/log/sinks/sync_frontend.hpp>
#include <boost/log/sinks/text_file_backend.hpp>
#include <boost/log/utility/setup/common_attributes.hpp>
#include <boost/log/expressions/keyword.hpp>

// Define the log levels
BOOST_LOG_ATTRIBUTE_KEYWORD(severity, "Severity", boost::log::trivial::severity_level)

// Set up the logging system
boost::log::core::get()->set_filter(boost::log::trivial::severity >= boost::log::trivial::info);
boost::log::add_console_log(std::cout, boost::log::keywords::format = "%TimeStamp% %Message%");

// Set up the file sink
boost::log::add_file_log("logs/myapp.log", boost::log::keywords::open_mode = std::ofstream::out | std::ofstream::app, boost::log::keywords::rotation_size = 1048576); // 1 MB

// Set up the remote logging service (using a network API)
boost::log::add_udp_server(std::string("localhost"), 3000);
```
In this example, we define three log levels: DEBUG, INFO, WARN, and ERROR. We also set up the logging system to use the `boost::log::trivial` module, which provides a pre-defined set of log attributes and filtering rules. The `boost::log::add_console_log()` function is used to output logs to the console, while the `boost::log::add_file_log()` function is used to output logs to a file. Finally, we set up the remote logging service using the `boost::log::add_udp_server()` function.

To configure the logging framework for a distributed microservices architecture, we can use a combination of environment variables and configuration files to control log levels, output targets, and error handling mechanisms. For example:
```bash
# Set the default log level for all services
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Configure the console sink for service1
CONSOLE_SINK=true
FILE_SINK=false
REMOTE_SINK=false

# Configure the file sink for service2
CONSOLE_SINK=false
FILE_SINK=true
FILE_PATH=/var/log/myapp/service2.log

# Configure the remote logging service for service3
CONSOLE_SINK=false
FILE_SINK=false
REMOTE_SINK=true
REMOTE_HOST=localhost
REMOTE_PORT=3000
```
In this example, we define three services (service1, service2, and service3) and configure the logging system for each service using environment variables. We set the default log level to INFO for all services, and configure the console sink for service1, the file sink for service2, and the remote logging service for service3. We also specify the output file path and host/port for the remote logging service.

To handle external service outages and fallback mechanisms, we can use a combination of error handling and retry logic. For example:
```cpp
// Define an error handler function to catch exceptions from the log sink
void error_handler(const boost::log::sinks::logger_error& err)
{
    if (err.code() == std::system_error_code(boost::log::sinks::errors::network_error))
    {
        // Handle network errors by retrying the log request after a delay
        boost::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
        boost::log::sinks::rethrow_exception(err);
    }
}

// Set up the remote logging service with an error handler function
boost::log::add_udp_server(std::string("localhost"), 3000, boost::log::keywords::on_error = &error_handler);
```
