    char message[]; // variable length message body
};
```
2. Logging Service: The logging service should be responsible for receiving, processing, and forwarding log entries to the external services. It should also provide real-time analytics capabilities, such as calculating average response times for specific API calls, identifying trends over time, and generating alerts based on predefined thresholds.
```c
void logging_service(const struct LogEntry *entry) {
    // Process the log entry
    process_log_entry(entry);

    // Forward the log entry to external services if necessary
    forward_log_entry(entry);
}
```
3. Log Entry Queue: The logging service should use a queue to store log entries until they can be processed and forwarded. This will help ensure reliable delivery of logs, even under heavy load.
```c
struct LogEntryQueue {
    struct LogEntry *entries[MAX_ENTRIES]; // Array of log entries
    int head; // Index of the next entry to process
    int tail; // Index of the last processed entry
};

int enqueue_log_entry(struct LogEntryQueue *queue, const struct LogEntry *entry) {
    if (queue->tail == MAX_ENTRIES - 1) {
        return -1; // Queue is full
    }

    queue->entries[queue->tail++] = entry;
    return 0; // Successfully enqueued the log entry
}
```
4. Log Entry Processing: The logging service should process each log entry, including extracting relevant information, calculating metrics, and generating alerts as necessary.
```c
void process_log_entry(const struct LogEntry *entry) {
    // Extract relevant information from the log entry
    const char *message = entry->message;
    int severity = entry->severity;

    // Calculate metrics based on the log entry
    switch (severity) {
        case INFO:
            // Log entry is an informational message
            break;
        case WARNING:
            // Log entry is a warning message
            break;
        case ERROR:
            // Log entry is an error message
            break;
    }

    // Generate alerts based on predefined thresholds
    if (severity >= WARNING) {
        generate_alert(message);
    }
}
```
5. Resource Monitoring: The logging service should use a resource monitoring tool to dynamically adjust logging resources (memory allocation, number of concurrent threads) based on current system load and resource availability. This will help optimize performance and prevent memory leaks or other issues.
```c
struct ResourceMonitor {
    float cpu_usage; // CPU usage percentage
    int memory_usage; // Memory usage in bytes
};

int monitor_resources(const struct ResourceMonitor *monitor) {
    if (monitor->cpu_usage > 80.0f || monitor->memory_usage > 9500000) {
        return -1; // Resources are exhausted
    }

    return 0; // Resources are available
}
```
6. Error Recovery: The logging service should implement comprehensive logging of failures, automatic retries with exponential backoff for transient network issues, and fallback strategies in case of prolonged failures with external services.
```c
void handle_error(const struct LogEntry *entry) {
    // Log the error
    log_error(entry);

    // Retry the failed operation with exponential backoff
    retry_operation(entry, 1000); // Initial delay of 1 second
}
```
7. Performance Profiling: The logging service should provide a built-in performance profiling tool that generates detailed reports on latency, throughput, and resource utilization. This will help developers identify and address bottlenecks in the system.
```c
struct PerformanceReport {
