### Example 2: Using the Builder Pattern

The builder pattern is often used to create complex objects that have many constructor parameters. The builder pattern allows for a more organized and readable way of constructing these objects.

Here's an example of using the builder pattern to create a `User` object with multiple constructor parameters.
```java
public class User {
    private final String name;
    private final int age;
    private final String email;

    public static class Builder {
        private String name;
        private int age;
        private String email;

        public Builder() {}

        public Builder withName(String name) {
            this.name = name;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder withAge(int age) {
            this.age = age;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder withEmail(String email) {
            this.email = email;
            return this;
        }

        public User build() {
            return new User(name, age, email);
        }
    }

    private User(String name, int age, String email) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        this.email = email;
    }
}
```
The `Builder` class contains several methods that start with the word `with`. These methods return the builder object after setting a specific property of the `User` object. The `build()` method returns an instance of the `User` class, which can then be used in your code.
```java
User user = new User.Builder()
        .withName("John")
        .withAge(25)
        .withEmail("john@example.com")
        .build();
```
In this example, the `User` object is created using the builder pattern. The `Builder` class contains methods for setting the name, age, and email properties of the `User` object. Once all the properties are set, the `build()` method returns an instance of the `User` class that can be used in your code.

### Example 3: Using a Facade Pattern

The facade pattern is often used to simplify complex APIs or libraries. The facade pattern provides a simple interface for interacting with the underlying library or API, while still allowing you to access all the features and functionality of the underlying library or API.
```java
public class MyFacade {
    private final Library lib;

    public MyFacade() {
        this.lib = new Library();
    }

    public void doSomething() {
        // call a method on the underlying library
        lib.doSomething();
    }
}
```
In this example, `MyFacade` provides a simple interface for interacting with the `Library`. The `MyFacade` class contains only one method, `doSomething`, which calls a method on the underlying `Library`. By using the facade pattern, you can simplify your code and make it easier to use the underlying library or API.
```java
MyFacade myFacade = new MyFacade();
myFacade.doSomething();
```
In this example, an instance of `MyFacade` is created and then the `doSomething()` method is called on it. This allows you to use the underlying library or API in a more simplified way, while still allowing you to access all its features and functionality.

### Example 4: Using a Decorator Pattern

The decorator pattern is often used to add new functionality to an object without modifying the existing code. The decorator pattern allows you to wrap an existing object with additional functionality, making it more powerful or flexible.
```java
public class FileLogger implements Logger {
    private final String filePath;

    public FileLogger(String filePath) {
        this.filePath = filePath;
    }

    @Override
    public void log(String message) {
        try (FileWriter fileWriter = new FileWriter(filePath)) {
            fileWriter.write(message);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // handle exception
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `FileLogger` class is a decorator for the `Logger` interface. The `FileLogger` class adds the functionality of writing messages to a file, while still allowing you to use the underlying `Logger` interface.
```java
Logger logger = new FileLogger("my-log-file.txt");
logger.log("Hello, world!");
```
In this example, an instance of `FileLogger` is created and then used as if it were a normal `Logger`. This allows you to add new functionality to the existing `Logger` interface without modifying the underlying code.

### Example 5: Using a Flyweight Pattern

The flyweight pattern is often used to reduce the memory usage of an application by sharing immutable objects. The flyweight pattern allows you to create multiple references to the same object, reducing the number of objects that need to be stored in memory.
```java
public class Word {
    private final String text;

    public Word(String text) {
        this.text = text;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object other) {
        if (other instanceof Word) {
            return ((Word) other).getText().equals(text);
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `Word` class is an immutable object that represents a word in a text. The `Word` class has a single property, `text`, which is the word's text. The `equals()` method is overridden to compare the words based on their text.
```java
// create two references to the same word
Word word1 = new Word("hello");
Word word2 = new Word("hello");

// check if they are equal
if (word1.equals(word2)) {
    System.out.println("The words are equal.");
} else {
    System.out.println("The words are not equal.");
}
```
In this example, two references to the same word are created and then compared using the `equals()` method. This allows you to compare multiple objects based on their text, even if they are different instances of the `Word` class.']

