### C++ Classes and Objects

C++ provides a way to create classes and objects that can encapsulate data and behavior. In the previous example, we used global functions to implement the logic of the program. However, in many cases it is more convenient to use object-oriented programming (OOP) techniques to organize the code.

In C++, a class is a blueprint for creating objects that have similar properties and methods. For example, if you wanted to create a class for representing a point in 2D space, you might define a constructor function that takes two arguments for the x-coordinate and y-coordinate of the point:
```c++
class Point {
public:
    int x;
    int y;

    // constructor function
    Point(int _x, int _y) : x(_x), y(_y) {}
};
```
You can then create objects of this class by calling the constructor function and passing in values for the x-coordinate and y-coordinate:
```c++
Point p1(3, 4); // creates a point with coordinates (3, 4)
```
Once you have created an object, you can access its properties using dot notation (e.g., `p1.x` and `p1.y`) and call its methods (e.g., `p1.print()`). In the previous example, we used a global function to implement the logic of the program. However, in many cases it is more convenient to use object-oriented programming techniques to organize the code.

For example, if you wanted to create a class for representing a rectangle in 2D space, you might define a constructor function that takes two arguments for the x-coordinate and y-coordinate of the top-left corner of the rectangle:
```c++
class Rectangle {
public:
    int x;
    int y;
    int width;
    int height;

    // constructor function
    Rectangle(int _x, int _y, int _width, int _height) : x(_x), y(_y), width(_width), height(_height) {}
};
```
You can then create objects of this class by calling the constructor function and passing in values for the x-coordinate and y-coordinate of the top-left corner of the rectangle:
```c++
Rectangle r1(3, 4, 5, 6); // creates a rectangle with coordinates (3, 4) and dimensions (5, 6)
```
Once you have created an object, you can access its properties using dot notation (e.g., `r1.x`, `r1.y`, `r1.width`, and `r1.height`) and call its methods (e.g., `r1.print()`).

### C++ Inheritance

Inheritance is a mechanism in which one class can inherit the properties and behavior of another class. This allows for code reuse and reduces duplication of code. For example, if you wanted to create a `Circle` class that inherits from a `Shape` class, you might define the constructor function as follows:
```c++
class Shape {
public:
    int x;
    int y;

    // constructor function
    Shape(int _x, int _y) : x(_x), y(_y) {}
};

class Circle : public Shape {
public:
    int radius;

    // constructor function
    Circle(int _x, int _y, int _radius) : Shape(_x, _y), radius(_radius) {}
};
```
In this example, the `Circle` class inherits from the `Shape` class. This means that all of the properties and methods of the `Shape` class are available to the `Circle` class as well. You can then create objects of these classes using the same constructor functions:
```c++
Shape s1(3, 4); // creates a shape with coordinates (3, 4)
Circle c1(3, 4, 5); // creates a circle with center (3, 4) and radius 5
```
### C++ Polymorphism

Polymorphism is the ability of an object to take on multiple forms. In C++, polymorphism can be implemented through method overriding and function overloading.

Method overriding is when a subclass provides its own implementation for a method that has the same name and signature as one of its superclass's methods. For example, if you wanted to create a `Rectangle` class that inherits from the `Shape` class, you might define a constructor function as follows:
```c++
class Shape {
public:
    int x;
    int y;

    // constructor function
    Shape(int _x, int _y) : x(_x), y(_y) {}
};

class Rectangle : public Shape {
public:
    int width;
    int height;

    // constructor function
    Rectangle(int _x, int _y, int _width, int _height) : Shape(_x, _y), width(_width), height(_height) {}
};
```
In this example, the `Rectangle` class inherits from the `Shape` class and adds its own properties (`width` and `height`). You can then create objects of these classes using the same constructor functions:
```c++
Shape s1(3, 4); // creates a shape with coordinates (3, 4)
Rectangle r1(3, 4, 5, 6); // creates a rectangle with center (3, 4), dimensions (5, 6), and same coordinates as s1
```
In this example, `r1` is both a `Shape` object and a `Rectangle` object. This allows for polymorphism, where the `r1` object can take on different forms depending on how it is used. For example, if you wanted to create a function that prints the coordinates of an object, you might define it as follows:
```c++
void printCoordinates(Shape& shape) {
    cout << "(" << shape.x << ", " << shape.y << ")";
}
```
You can then call this function on objects of different classes:
```c++
printCoordinates(s1); // prints (3, 4)
printCoordinates(r1); // prints (3, 4), same as s1
```
Function overloading is when two or more functions have the same name but differ in their parameters. In C++, you can use this to implement polymorphism. For example, if you wanted to create a `Rectangle` class that inherits from the `Shape` class, you might define a function that takes an object of the `Rectangle` class as an argument and prints its coordinates:
```c++
class Shape {
public:
    int x;
    int y;

    // constructor function
    Shape(int _x, int _y) : x(_x), y(_y) {}
};

class Rectangle : public Shape {
public:
    int width;
    int height;

    // constructor function
    Rectangle(int _x, int _y, int _width, int _height) : Shape(_x, _y), width(_width), height(_height) {}
};
```
In this example, the `Rectangle` class inherits from the `Shape` class and adds its own properties (`width` and `height`). You can then create objects of these classes using the same constructor functions:
```c++
Shape s1(3, 4); // creates a shape with coordinates (3, 4)
Rectangle r1(3, 4, 5, 6); // creates a rectangle with center (3, 4), dimensions (5, 6), and same coordinates as s1
```
You can then define a function that takes an object of the `Rectangle` class as an argument and prints its coordinates:
```c++
void printCoordinates(Rectangle& rect) {
    cout << "(" << rect.x << ", " << rect.y << ")";
}
```
You can then call this function on objects of different classes:
```c++
printCoordinates(s1); // prints (3, 4), the coordinates of s1
printCoordinates(r1); // prints (3, 4), the same as s1
```
In this example, `printCoordinates` is overloaded to take an object of either the `Shape` class or the `Rectangle` class. This allows for polymorphism, where the function can be called on objects of different classes and it will produce the appropriate output depending on what type of object it is.']

