To implement the scalable C library, we will use a singly linked list data structure to store the `item` structures. Each `item` will contain a pointer to another `item` structure, forming a linked list. The `item` structures will also include a `char*` name and a `float` value field.
```c
typedef struct item {
    char* name;
    float value;
    struct item* next;
} item_t;
```
To initialize the list with `item` instances from a provided `item_data` array, we will use a loop to iterate through the array and dynamically allocate memory for each `name` field. We will also set the `next` pointer of each `item` structure to point to the next `item` in the linked list.
```c
int main(void) {
    // Initialize the item data array
    item_t* item_data = (item_t*) malloc(sizeof(item_t) * 5);

    // Fill the item data array with items
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        item_data[i].name = "Item Name";
        item_data[i].value = 1.0f;
        item_data[i].next = &item_data[i + 1];
    }

    // Create the linked list of items
    item_t* head = &item_data[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) {
        item_t* current = &head[i - 1];
        current->next = &head[i];
    }
}
```
To clean up the memory allocated for the `item` structures, we will use a thread-safe function to release all of the memory. We will use the `pthread_rwlock_t` type to create a read-write lock that can be used to synchronize access to the linked list and ensure that only one thread is modifying it at a time.
```c
void cleanup(item_t* head) {
    // Create a read-write lock for synchronizing access to the linked list
    pthread_rwlock_t rwlock;
    pthread_rwlock_init(&rwlock, NULL);

    // Acquire a write lock on the linked list
    pthread_rwlock_wrlock(&rwlock);

    // Release all of the memory allocated for the `item` structures
    while (head != NULL) {
        item_t* next = head->next;
        free(head);
        head = next;
    }

    // Release the read-write lock on the linked list
    pthread_rwlock_unlock(&rwlock);
}
```
To integrate with an existing microservices architecture that uses a distributed caching system for shared state, we will use a combination of data sharing and caching strategies. We will cache frequently accessed items in memory to reduce the number of requests made to the underlying data storage system. We will also share data between services using messaging protocols such as RabbitMQ or Apache Kafka.
```c
void shared_state(item_t* head) {
    // Create a message queue for sharing state between services
    MessageQueue queue = createMessageQueue();

    // Fill the queue with messages containing items from the linked list
    while (head != NULL) {
        item_t* current = head;
        while (current != NULL) {
            char* message = serializeItem(current);
            enqueue(queue, message);
            current = current->next;
        }
    }

    // Share the messages between services using a messaging protocol
    shareMessages(queue);
}
```
To implement real-time data analytics capabilities, we will use machine learning models to detect anomalies and trigger alerts based on predefined thresholds or rules. We will train the models on historical data and deploy them within the service, ensuring that they are updated regularly with new data to maintain accuracy and relevance.
```c
void predictive_analytics(item_t* head) {
    // Train machine learning models on historical data
    Model model = trainModels(head);

    // Use the trained models to detect anomalies in real-time
    while (head != NULL) {
        item_t* current = head;
        while (current != NULL) {
            float value = current->value;
            if (isAnomaly(model, value)) {
                // Trigger alerts based on predefined thresholds or rules
                triggerAlerts(value);
            }
            current = current->next;
        }
    }
}
```
To optimize resource allocation and consider auto-scaling policies, we will use cloud providers to manage the underlying infrastructure. We will also implement redundancy mechanisms to mitigate risks associated with single points of failure.
```c
void cloud_infrastructure(item_t* head) {
    // Use cloud providers to manage the underlying infrastructure
    CloudProvider provider = createCloudProvider();

    // Create a load balancer to distribute requests across multiple instances
    LoadBalancer loadbalancer = createLoadBalancer();

    // Create an auto-scaling policy to adjust the number of instances in response to changing loads
    AutoScalingPolicy scaling_policy = createAutoScalingPolicy(head);
}
```
To ensure compliance with relevant data protection regulations, we will implement data retention policies, anonymization techniques, and pseudonymization strategies. We will also provide tools for users to exercise their rights under these regulations, including data portability and deletion options.
```c
void data_protection(item_t* head) {
    // Implement data retention policies to ensure compliance with relevant data protection regulations
    int retention_period = 10; // in days
    while (head != NULL) {
        item_t* current = head;
        while (current != NULL) {
            char* message = serializeItem(current);
            storeMessage(message, retention_period);
            current = current->next;
        }
    }
}
```
