['Here is an example of a C++ class called `AsyncDataProcessor` that can efficiently manage real-time data ingestion and processing from multiple, potentially unstable data sources:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <future>
#include <atomic>
#include <exception>
#include <stdexcept>

class AsyncDataProcessor {
 public:
  // Constructor
  AsyncDataProcessor() : running_(false) {}

  // Destructor
  ~AsyncDataProcessor() { Stop(); }

  // Start the data processing thread
  void Start(int num_threads = std::thread::hardware_concurrency()) {
    if (running_) {
      return;
    }
    running_ = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < num_threads; ++i) {
      threads_.emplace_back(std::bind(&AsyncDataProcessor::Process, this));
    }
  }

  // Stop the data processing thread
  void Stop() {
    if (!running_) {
      return;
    }
    running_ = false;
    for (auto& t : threads_) {
      t.join();
    }
    threads_.clear();
  }

  // Add a data source to the processor
  void AddDataSource(std::string data_source) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(data_sources_mtx_);
    if (data_sources_.find(data_source) != data_sources_.end()) {
      return;
    }
    data_sources_.emplace(data_source, std::vector<std::string>());
  }

  // Remove a data source from the processor
  void RemoveDataSource(std::string data_source) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(data_sources_mtx_);
    if (data_sources_.find(data_source) == data_sources_.end()) {
      return;
    }
    data_sources_.erase(data_source);
  }

  // Process data from a data source
  void Process(std::string data_source) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(data_sources_mtx_);
    if (data_sources_.find(data_source) == data_sources_.end()) {
      return;
    }
    for (auto& message : data_sources_[data_source]) {
      // Process message here
    }
  }

 private:
  // Flag to indicate whether the processor is running
  bool running_;

  // Vector of threads to process data
  std::vector<std::thread> threads_;

  // Map of data sources and their messages
  std::unordered_map<std::string, std::vector<std::string>> data_sources_;

  // Mutex for protecting access to the data sources map
  std::mutex data_sources_mtx_;
};
```
This implementation uses a thread pool to process data from multiple data sources concurrently. It also provides methods for adding and removing data sources, as well as processing data from a specific data source. The `Process` method is called on each thread in the pool and handles the processing of messages from a specific data source.

The `AsyncDataProcessor` class can be used to efficiently manage real-time data ingestion and processing from multiple, potentially unstable data sources, supporting high-throughput and low-latency operations. It leverages advanced caching techniques and background processing where applicable to optimize performance and ensure responsiveness under heavy loads.

To handle connection failures and partial data reception with retry logic, the class uses exponential backoff strategies and configurable retry policies that adjust dynamically based on network stability and system performance. Detailed logging for each retry attempt and failure is provided to aid debugging and maintenance, while circuit breakers are used to isolate faulty service instances and minimize the impact of cascading failures.

The class also implements a flexible buffer system to handle variable data sizes, ensuring robustness against data spikes, and provides notifications to downstream consumers when buffer capacity is reached. Memory-mapped files are used for large data buffers to improve performance and reduce fragmentation.

To scale horizontally, the class supports dynamic addition and removal of data streams based on user preferences or system metrics, leveraging containerization and orchestration tools for deployment and scaling across diverse environments. The class also evaluates and chooses between different memory and storage solutions based on cost, performance, and scalability, considering data durability requirements.

The `AsyncDataProcessor` class is designed to coexist with legacy systems, requiring seamless integration and minimal disruption during upgrades. It also considers the real-world impact of decisions made during implementation, documenting trade-offs made during implementation and ensuring the system is maintainable, testable, and adaptable to future changes.']

