  std::vector<WSConnection> connections = {{"ws://example.com", 80, true}, {"ws://example2.com", 80, false}};

  // Create a NetworkManager object with the list of WebSocket connections
  NetworkManager manager(connections);

  // Connect to all WebSocket connections
  manager.connect();

  // Send a message to all open WebSocket connections
  std::string message = "Hello, World!";
  manager.sendMessage(message, [](const WSMessage& msg) {
    std::cout << "Received message: " << msg.data << std::endl;
  });

  // Receive a message from any of the open WebSocket connections
  manager.receiveMessage([](const WSMessage& msg) {
    std::cout << "Received message: " << msg.data << std::endl;
  });

  // Disconnect from all WebSocket connections
  manager.disconnect();
}
```
This example creates a `NetworkManager` object with a list of WebSocket connections, connects to them, sends a message to all open connections, and receives messages from any of the open connections. After disconnecting from the WebSocket connections, the `NetworkManager` destructor is called, which closes the WebSocket connections and deallocates memory.

To optimize the performance of the `NetworkManager`, you can use techniques such as:

* Using efficient data serialization formats (e.g., Protocol Buffers, MessagePack) to reduce memory allocations and I/O overhead.
