The `init_cipher_context` function initializes the cipher context by generating a cryptographically secure random initialization vector (IV) and deriving the encryption and decryption keys using PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256.

The `update_keys` function updates the encryption and decryption keys using PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256. This is useful when the password is changed, as it allows to update the keys in the cipher context without having to reinitialize the entire context.

The `encrypt` function encrypts data using AES-256-GCM. It generates a new IV for each encryption operation and uses PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256 to initialize the encryption key. The encrypted data is then returned as a pointer to the ciphertext.

The `decrypt` function decrypts data using AES-256-GCM. It generates a new IV for each decryption operation and uses PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256 to initialize the decryption key. The decrypted data is then returned as a pointer to the plaintext.

The `process` function processes data using the cipher context by encrypting it, then decrypting it again. This simulates the actual usage of the encryption and decryption functions in a real-world scenario.

In the main function, we first initialize the cipher context and process some sample data using it. We then update the keys using PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256 and process the same data again using the updated cipher context. This demonstrates how the system can be used to handle password changes in a secure way by updating the keys without having to reinitialize the entire context.']

